  "title": "Service Discovery Fundamentals",
  "description": "Understand why service discovery is needed in microservices.",
  "order": 1,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 16,
  "tags": ["service-discovery", "fundamentals"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "In a microservices architecture, services need to find each other. Hardcoding IP addresses does not work because services scale, fail, and move. Service discovery solves this by providing a registry of available service instances."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Key Concepts"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "Service Registry",
            "definition": "A database of available service instances and their network locations."
          },
          {
            "term": "Service Instance",
            "definition": "A running copy of a service."
          },
          {
            "term": "Service Discovery",
            "definition": "The process of finding the network location of a service instance."
          },
          {
            "term": "DNS Discovery",
            "definition": "Using DNS to resolve service names to addresses."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Why Service Discovery"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Services scale up and down dynamically",
          "Instances move due to failures and deployments",
          "IP addresses change frequently",
          "Hardcoding addresses is impossible"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Components"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Service Registry: stores instance locations",
          "Service Provider: registers instances",
          "Service Consumer: discovers instances",
          "Health Checks: remove unhealthy instances"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Discovery Patterns"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": ["Pattern", "Description", "Example"],
        "rows": [
          ["Client-side", "Client queries registry directly", "Netflix Eureka, Consul"],
          ["Server-side", "Load balancer queries registry", "AWS ALB, Kubernetes"],
          ["DNS-based", "Client uses DNS to discover", "Kubernetes DNS, Consul DNS"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Architecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "msa-service-discovery",
        "caption": "Service Discovery Flow",
        "definition": "flowchart TD\n    SvcA[Service A] -->|Register| Registry[(Service Registry)]\n    SvcB[Service B] -->|Register| Registry\n    SvcC[Service C] -->|Discover| Registry\n    Registry -->|Instance List| SvcC\n    SvcC -->|Call| SvcA\n    SvcC -->|Call| SvcB"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Best Practices"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Design for failure",
          "Keep services loosely coupled",
          "Monitor and observe everything",
          "Automate testing and deployment"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
